## Option 1 — Board-ready: "Specify it before the concrete does"

**Thesis (one sentence).** MWAA should adopt a Terminal Acoustic and
Information Performance Standard as a Design Manual amendment now, because the
largest terminal-construction pipeline in the authority's history is about to
harden its designs, and the same requirements cost 0.06–0.20% of the IAD
program written early versus a 2–4× retrofit premium bought back late.

**Communication job.** By the end, the MWAA Board and capital-planning leaders
should authorize a design-manual amendment on the current CCP procurement
clock, because the standard is cheap now, expensive later, and impossible to
retrofit into a certified life-safety system once designs freeze.

**Opening scene.** Concourse E, late 2026: 435,000 square feet, 14 United
gates, 46,000 square feet of concessions — the first delivered piece of the
Dulles modernization, and the last major terminal space MWAA will open without
having decided what its hold rooms should sound like. Behind it: a $9.0 B CCP
and an announced $20–22.5 B transformation touching more than 5 million square
feet.

**Argument sequence (7 beats).**

1. *The pipeline is the opportunity.* The July 2026 IAD announcement plus the
   $9 B CCP create the longest run of unhardened terminal design MWAA will
   ever govern at once.
   — `infrastructure-economist::ev-ea35cdc1f28c`,
   `operations-analyst::ev-c5bc4ad4021f`,
   `infrastructure-economist::ev-c76972ceca45`
2. *The floors already exist in code.* NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 average / ≥ 0.45 at
   90% of points; Part 382 accessible flight-critical information; ADA Title II
   "as effective" communication. These are not aspirations; they are law.
   — `operations-analyst::ev-0b02c79dde7f`,
   `regulatory-political-analyst::ev-63adc52d8642`,
   `contrarian::ev-d8c021903d7c`, `regulatory-political-analyst::ev-ada35160`
3. *Answer the Contrarian head-on.* If codes already require this, why a
   standard? Because the requirement lives in integration — acoustics, PA
   zoning, FIDS, tenant systems, and commissioning are specified by different
   parties on different contracts, and nobody currently owns the seam.
   — Contrarian brief vs. COO/CEO/economist cluster (evidence-map §3 tension)
4. *What integration failure costs.* Berlin Brandenburg: a PA-adjacent
   life-safety integration that never certified against a frozen design,
   ~€2 B → ~€7.3 B and an eight-year drag. Denver's baggage system: same
   structure, different subsystem.
   — `infrastructure-economist::ev-fb4c8ac3e4c2`,
   `infrastructure-economist::ev-b09e4cfcff40`,
   `infrastructure-economist::ev-540cace0221c`
5. *What early specification costs.* $5–15/sf new versus $18–45/sf retrofit;
   applied to Concourse E, $1.3 M–$17.4 M avoided rework; MWAA-wide stack
   $12 M–$40 M one-time, 0.16–0.54% of projected 2030 CPE.
   — Quantitative exhibits E1, E2, E6; 1:10:100 direction (not magnitude) per
   `infrastructure-economist` caveats
6. *The affordability envelope makes timing binding.* CPE $12.77 → $20.71 by
   2030; ~$5.5 B new debt 2025–2028; debt per O&D enplanement peaking near
   $400. There is headroom for an early spec, not for a late one.
   — `infrastructure-economist::ev-711bbeadb6f5`,
   `airport-ceo::ev-3fd3e1b4aa7b`
7. *The decision.* A tiered standard — mandatory intelligibility and
   redundancy floor, recommended practices, context-dependent options with a
   DCA variance — entering the Design Manual, consultant scopes, tenant
   volumes, and commissioning per IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions.
   — Evidence-map §10 handoff; `airport-coo::ev-34d980653e2f`

**Strongest objection and where it enters.** The Contrarian's: existing codes
plus competent commissioning already require everything the standard would
require; a new document is governance overhead. It enters at beat 3, early and
by name, and the whole middle of the argument is the answer.

**Analogy.** None required. The BER and DEN cases do the comparative work as
literal precedents; adding a cross-domain analogy would dilute a deliberately
conservative frame.

**Signature visual.** A single timing chart: specification cost per square
foot at design ($5–15) versus retrofit ($18–45), with Concourse E's 435,000 sq
ft and the announced 5 M+ sq ft pipeline plotted as the stakes, and the E6
stack shown as a 0.06–0.20% sliver of the IAD program.

**Airport decision this leads toward.** Board direction to amend the Airports
Authority Design Manual with a tiered acoustic and information performance
standard, applied to all CCP and transformation-program design contracts from
the next procurement forward.

**Risk of this frame.** Predictability. It is the article the audience expects
from the title, and the run prompt's audience "has read McKinsey decks and is
tired of them." Its safety is also its ceiling: it under-uses the ledger's two
most surprising findings (DCA's higher floor; MWAA not owning the microphone).

